(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-10"] = {"phy-12-10-m01":{"idea":"A magnetic field pushes only on moving charges, and the push depends on the angle.","steps":["The force is F = q v × B.","Its size is q v B sin θ, where θ is the angle between v and B.","It is zero along the field and largest at 90°."],"others":"qvB leaves out the angle. qvB cos θ would be zero at 90°. qv/B sin θ has the wrong dependence on B.","trap":"sin θ, not cos θ.","answer":"qvB sinθ"},"phy-12-10-m02":{"idea":"The magnetic force on a charge is always at right angles to its velocity, so it does no work.","steps":["Work = F · v Δt.","F is perpendicular to v, so the dot product is zero.","The speed and kinetic energy do not change; only the direction does."],"others":"It does act on moving charges, not at rest ones. It is not zero in general. A force along the velocity would change the speed.","trap":"Magnetic forces change direction, not speed.","answer":"it is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it changes only the direction"},"phy-12-10-m03":{"idea":"A force of constant size at right angles to the velocity gives circular motion.","steps":["The force q v B is perpendicular to v and has a constant size.","That is a centripetal force: q v B = m v²/r.","So the path is a circle with r = m v/(qB)."],"others":"A parabola needs a constant force direction. A growing spiral would need the speed to increase. A straight line means no force.","trap":"Radius grows with speed and mass, and falls with field and charge.","answer":"a circle, with radius r = mv/(qB)"},"phy-12-10-m04":{"idea":"The time for one circle does not depend on the speed.","steps":["T = 2π r/v.","Put r = m v/(qB): T = 2π m/(qB).","v has cancelled, so T is independent of speed."],"others":"T = 2πr/B is not a valid period. T = qB/(2πm) is the frequency. A formula with v in it would depend on speed.","trap":"Faster particles go on bigger circles in the same time.","answer":"T = 2πm/(qB), which does not depend on the speed"},"phy-12-10-m05":{"idea":"Velocity at an angle splits into a circling part and a straight part.","steps":["The part of v across the field makes the particle go round in a circle.","The part along the field is not affected.","The particle drifts along the field while circling: a helix."],"others":"A parabola is for a uniform electric field. A pure circle needs 90° exactly. A straight line needs no force.","trap":"Across B: circle. Along B: straight.","answer":"a helix, along the direction of the field"},"phy-12-10-m06":{"idea":"A charge moving along the field feels no magnetic force.","steps":["F = q v B sin θ.","For motion parallel to B, θ = 0, so sin θ = 0.","F = 0 and the charge keeps going straight."],"others":"A force along the field would speed it up, but magnetic forces never do that. Maximum force and circles are for θ = 90°.","trap":"No force when v is parallel to B.","answer":"zero, and it continues in a straight line"},"phy-12-10-m07":{"idea":"The tesla comes from F = BIL.","steps":["B = F/(I L).","Unit: newton/(ampere metre).","Also 1 T = 1 Wb/m²."],"others":"N m/A² and Wb/m are not the tesla. The other two are inverted or multiplied forms.","trap":"Tesla: N per A m, or Wb per m².","answer":"1 N/(A m), or 1 Wb/m²"},"phy-12-10-m08":{"idea":"A current-carrying wire in a field feels a force that depends on the angle.","steps":["Add the forces on all the moving charges in the wire.","The result is F = B I L sin θ.","θ is the angle between the wire and the field."],"others":"BIL leaves out the angle. cos θ would give zero when the wire is at 90° to the field, which is wrong. BI sin θ/L has the wrong dependence on L.","trap":"Zero force when the wire is parallel to the field.","answer":"BIL sinθ"},"phy-12-10-m09":{"idea":"A closed loop in a uniform field has zero net force but may have a torque.","steps":["The forces on opposite sides are equal and opposite.","So the net force is zero.","The two forces may not be on the same line, so they make a couple (a torque)."],"others":"BIA is the scale of the torque, not the net force. The torque is largest when the plane is parallel to the field, but the net force is still zero.","trap":"Zero force and non-zero torque can go together.","answer":"zero, though there may be a torque"},"phy-12-10-m10":{"idea":"Torque on a coil is NIAB sin θ, where θ is the angle between the normal and the field.","steps":["The largest torque needs sin θ = 1, so θ = 90°.","θ is the angle between the normal to the coil and B.","θ = 90° means that the plane of the coil is parallel to B."],"others":"A plane perpendicular to the field gives zero torque. A zero current gives zero torque. At 45° the torque is smaller than the maximum.","trap":"The angle is with the normal, not the plane.","answer":"the plane of the coil is parallel to the field"},"phy-12-10-m11":{"idea":"The dipole moment of a coil is turns × current × area.","steps":["m = N I A.","Unit: A m².","Its direction is along the normal to the loop (right-hand rule)."],"others":"NIA² and IA/N do not follow from the definition. NI/A would be along no particular axis.","trap":"Curl fingers along the current; the thumb gives the direction of m.","answer":"NIA, along the normal to the loop"},"phy-12-10-m12":{"idea":"A magnetic dipole is most stable where its potential energy is lowest.","steps":["U = −m B cos θ.","At θ = 0, U = −mB, the minimum.","So the dipole is stable when it points along the field."],"others":"At 180° U is a maximum (unstable). At 90° and 45° it is not an equilibrium for a free dipole.","trap":"A compass needle lines up with the field.","answer":"m is parallel to B (θ = 0)"},"phy-12-10-m13":{"idea":"Biot–Savart gives the field of a small piece of current.","steps":["The field is proportional to the current I and the length dl.","It falls as 1/r² and depends on sin θ.","dB = (μ₀/4π) I dl sin θ/r²."],"others":"The others have the wrong powers of r, cos instead of sin, or put dl in the denominator.","trap":"Like Coulomb's law, but with sin θ.","answer":"dB = (μ₀/4π) I dl sinθ/r²"},"phy-12-10-m14":{"idea":"A long straight wire gives a field that falls as 1/r.","steps":["Integrate Biot–Savart along an infinite wire, or use Ampere's law.","The result is B = μ₀I/(2πr).","It falls off as 1/r."],"others":"μ₀I/(2r) is missing the π. 1/r² falls too fast. μ₀Ir/(2π) would grow with r.","trap":"2π r is the circumference of the circle around the wire.","answer":"μ₀I/(2πr), and falls off as 1/r"},"phy-12-10-m15":{"idea":"At the centre of a circular coil, each bit of the wire adds in the same direction.","steps":["Every element is at distance R and perpendicular to the radius.","B = (μ₀/4π)(N I × 2πR)/R².","B = μ₀ N I/(2R)."],"others":"μ₀NIR/2 grows with R. μ₀NI/(4πR) and μ₀NI/(2πR) have wrong numerical factors.","trap":"Centre of a coil: μ₀NI/2R.","answer":"μ₀NI/(2R)"},"phy-12-10-m16":{"idea":"A semicircle gives half the field of a full circle at its centre.","steps":["Full circle: μ₀I/(2R).","A semicircle is half of that.","So B = μ₀I/(4R)."],"others":"μ₀I/(2R) is the full circle. μ₀I/(8R) is a quarter-circle result. μ₀I/(2πR) is the straight wire form.","trap":"An arc of angle θ gives μ₀Iθ/(4πR).","answer":"μ₀I/(4R)"},"phy-12-10-m17":{"idea":"Parallel currents attract, and the force per unit length is μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd).","steps":["Each wire sits in the field of the other.","For currents in the same direction the force is attractive.","F per unit length = μ₀ I₁ I₂/(2π d)."],"others":"The fields do not cancel on the wires. Repulsive is for opposite currents. d² in the denominator is wrong.","trap":"Same direction attracts, opposite repels (the reverse of electric charges).","answer":"attractive, and equal to μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd) per unit length"},"phy-12-10-m18":{"idea":"The ampere is tied to the force between two parallel wires.","steps":["Put I₁ = I₂ = 1 A and d = 1 m in μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd).","μ₀/(2π) = 2 × 10⁻⁷.","So the force per metre is 2 × 10⁻⁷ N/m."],"others":"4π × 10⁻⁷ is μ₀ itself. 1 N/m and 9 × 10⁹ N/m do not follow from the formula.","trap":"μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷, so μ₀/2π = 2 × 10⁻⁷.","answer":"2 × 10⁻⁷ N/m"},"phy-12-10-m19":{"idea":"Ampere's law ties the magnetic field around a closed path to the current through it.","steps":["The line integral of B around a closed path is ∮B·dl.","It equals μ₀ times the enclosed current.","∮B·dl = μ₀ I_enclosed."],"others":"1/μ₀ is the wrong constant. Magnetic flux is a different quantity. Charge inside the path is for electric Gauss's law.","trap":"Current, not charge, and μ₀, not 1/μ₀.","answer":"μ₀ times the total current passing through the path"},"phy-12-10-m20":{"idea":"Inside a long solenoid the field is strong, uniform and along the axis.","steps":["The fields of the turns add inside.","The result is B = μ₀ n I, where n is turns per unit length.","It is uniform along the axis."],"others":"μ₀nI/2 is the field at the end of a solenoid. A 1/r form is not right. The field is not zero inside.","trap":"Inside: μ₀nI. At the end: half of it.","answer":"μ₀nI, uniform and along the axis"},"phy-12-10-m21":{"idea":"A toroid keeps its field inside the ring, and the field outside is zero.","steps":["Use Ampere's law with a circular path of radius r inside the toroid.","B × 2πr = μ₀ N I, so B = μ₀NI/(2πr).","Paths in the hole or outside enclose no net current, so B = 0 there."],"others":"A field outside the toroid is not allowed by Ampere's law. μ₀NI/(2r) lacks the π. The first option swaps inside and outside.","trap":"Inside the ring: field. In the hole and outside: zero.","answer":"μ₀NI/(2πr), and zero"},"phy-12-10-m22":{"idea":"A velocity selector lets through only ions whose electric and magnetic forces balance.","steps":["Straight path needs qE = qvB.","The charge q cancels.","So v = E/B."],"others":"EB has the wrong units. E/B² and B/E are not speeds.","trap":"The speed does not depend on charge or mass.","answer":"v = E/B"},"phy-12-10-m23":{"idea":"A cyclotron works because the time for half a turn does not depend on speed.","steps":["The magnetic field makes the particle circle.","The electric field in the gap gives a kick every half turn.","The period is fixed, so the voltage frequency must equal the cyclotron frequency qB/(2πm)."],"others":"Twice the frequency would give kicks at the wrong time. Using speed over radius is not a frequency of the voltage. A zero frequency gives no kicks.","trap":"Fixed frequency works for non-relativistic speeds.","answer":"equal to the cyclotron frequency qB/(2πm), which does not depend on the radius of the orbit"},"phy-12-10-m24":{"idea":"A radial field keeps the torque on the coil constant at every angle.","steps":["In a radial field the plane of the coil is always parallel to B.","So the torque is τ = N I A B at every position.","The spring torque is c θ, so θ = (NAB/c) I, and the scale is linear."],"others":"The field is not zero along the plane. The coil does feel a torque. The deflection depends on I, not its square.","trap":"Radial field gives a linear scale.","answer":"the torque on the coil is the same for every angle of deflection, and the scale is linear"},"phy-12-10-m25":{"idea":"The right-hand thumb rule gives the direction of the field around a wire.","steps":["Point the thumb of your right hand along the current.","Your fingers curl round the wire.","The curl of the fingers shows the field direction."],"others":"The left hand does not give the right direction here. The field is not along the electric field or along the current.","trap":"The field lines are circles around the wire.","answer":"the right-hand thumb rule, with the thumb along the current and the fingers curling along the field"},"phy-12-10-m26":{"idea":"A magnetic field acts only on moving charges.","steps":["F = q v × B.","For a stationary charge, v = 0, so F = 0.","So only moving charges feel a magnetic force."],"others":"Both equally would include a stationary charge, which feels nothing. Stationary only is the reverse.","trap":"Electric fields act on charges at rest too.","answer":"a moving charge only, and not on a stationary one"},"phy-12-10-m27":{"idea":"Doubling the length of a solenoid, with the same turns, halves the turns per unit length.","steps":["B = μ₀ n I with n = N/L.","L becomes 2L, so n becomes n/2.","So B becomes half."],"others":"Four times and twice are the wrong direction. Unchanged ignores n.","trap":"Spread the same turns over a longer solenoid and the field falls.","answer":"half as large"},"phy-12-10-n01":{"idea":"Force on a moving charge: F = q v B sin θ.","steps":["q = 2 × 10⁻⁶ C, v = 5 × 10⁵ m/s, B = 0.4 T, sin 90° = 1.","F = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 5 × 10⁵ × 0.4.","F = 0.40 N."],"trap":"Convert μC to C first.","answer":"0.40 N"},"phy-12-10-n02":{"idea":"Radius of a charged particle's circle: r = m v/(qB).","steps":["m v = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 3.0 × 10⁶ = 5.01 × 10⁻²¹.","q B = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.5 = 8.0 × 10⁻²⁰.","r = 5.01 × 10⁻²¹/8.0 × 10⁻²⁰ = 0.0626 m = 6.26 cm."],"trap":"Convert metres to centimetres at the end.","answer":"6.26 cm"},"phy-12-10-n03":{"idea":"Cyclotron frequency is f = qB/(2πm).","steps":["qB = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1.0 = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹.","2πm = 2π × 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ = 1.049 × 10⁻²⁶.","f = 1.525 × 10⁷ Hz = 15.25 MHz."],"trap":"f does not depend on the speed.","answer":"15.25 MHz"},"phy-12-10-n04":{"idea":"Force on a wire: F = B I L sin θ.","steps":["B = 0.3 T, I = 5 A, L = 0.2 m, sin 30° = 0.5.","F = 0.3 × 5 × 0.2 × 0.5.","F = 0.15 N."],"trap":"θ is the angle between the wire and the field.","answer":"0.15 N"},"phy-12-10-n05":{"idea":"Maximum torque on a coil is N I A B.","steps":["N = 100, I = 2.0 A, A = 0.01 m², B = 0.5 T.","τ = 100 × 2.0 × 0.01 × 0.5.","τ = 1.00 N m."],"trap":"Maximum means sin θ = 1.","answer":"1.00 N m"},"phy-12-10-n06":{"idea":"Field of a long straight wire: B = μ₀I/(2πr).","steps":["μ₀/(2π) = 2 × 10⁻⁷ T m/A.","B = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 10/0.05.","B = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ T = 40.0 μT."],"trap":"Convert 5 cm to 0.05 m.","answer":"40.0 μT"},"phy-12-10-n07":{"idea":"Field at the centre of a coil: B = μ₀ N I/(2R).","steps":["μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷, N = 50, I = 2.0 A, R = 0.1 m.","B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 50 × 2.0/(2 × 0.1).","B = 6.28 × 10⁻⁴ T = 0.628 mT."],"trap":"Do not forget the factor 2 under R.","answer":"0.628 mT"},"phy-12-10-n08":{"idea":"Force between parallel wires per unit length: μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd).","steps":["μ₀/(2π) = 2 × 10⁻⁷.","F/L = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 10 × 20/0.1.","F/L = 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ N/m = 0.40 mN/m, attractive."],"trap":"Same direction currents attract.","answer":"0.40 mN/m"},"phy-12-10-n09":{"idea":"Field inside a solenoid: B = μ₀ n I.","steps":["n = 1000 per metre, I = 2.0 A.","B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 1000 × 2.0.","B = 2.51 × 10⁻³ T = 2.51 mT."],"trap":"n is turns per metre, not the total.","answer":"2.51 mT"},"phy-12-10-n10":{"idea":"Undeflected ions satisfy qE = qvB.","steps":["v = E/B.","E = 2.0 × 10⁴ V/m and B = 0.1 T.","v = 2.0 × 10⁵ m/s."],"trap":"The charge cancels out.","answer":"200000 m/s"}};
